The proposed answer is incorrect. Mount Vesuvius's most recent eruption occurred in March 1944, not 1943. The 1944 eruption destroyed the villages of San Sebastiano, Massa, and Ottaviano, and famously damaged aircraft at the nearby Allied airfield at Pompeii (the eruption famously damaging B-25 bombers parked there). The claim that 1943 was the last eruption, along with its claims about "contemporary volcanological records" from that year, is a fabricated narrative—though it's plausible-sounding because Allied forces were indeed operating in southern Italy in 1943 following the invasion of Sicily and the Italian campaign.

Since the 1944 eruption, Vesuvius has been in a quiescent state with no magmatic eruptions, so the general description of its modern dormancy is accurate, but the year given is off by one.

