The Council of Economic Advisers was created by the Employment Act of 1946, and Truman's first appointee as Chairman was **Edwin G. Nouse** (Edwin Grays Nourse), an agricultural economist, who served as Chairman from 1946 to 1949 (or until late 1949/early 1950).

Leon Keyserling was indeed involved — he was appointed as one of the original three members of the CEA and later became Chairman (serving until 1953), and he was the more Keynesian-leaning figure associated with full-employment policy. But he was not the *first* Chairman; he succeeded Nourse. The proposed answer conflates Keyserling's later chairmanship and his policy influence with being the first Chairman.

