The proposed answer conflates two patients. Barnard's first human-to-human heart transplant on 3 December 1967 was performed on **Louis Washkansky**, a 54-year-old grocer from Cape Town, whose failing heart was replaced with that of Denise Darvall, a young woman killed in a car accident. Washkansky survived 18 days before dying of pneumonia/immune complications.

Philip Blaiberg was the **recipient of the second transplant**, performed in January 1968, and became famous because he survived much longer (about 19 months), making him the most publicly visible early transplant patient — which likely explains the confusion in the proposed answer. The details in the answer (fatal accident donor, early hours of 3 December 1967) actually fit Washkansky's case, not Blaiberg's.

